# Rank 1083 / CVE-2024-47875: DOMPurify placed no limit on element nesting
# depth, so deeply nested markup made the browser's parser re-serialize the tree
# in a way that reversed sanitization (CWE-79, mutation XSS). The fix 6ea80cd8b4
# adds MAX_NESTING_DEPTH = 500 to src/purify.js, tracks __depth and
# __removalCount per node in _sanitizeElements and _sanitizeShadowDOM, removes
# any element at or past that depth, and adds both new properties to the
# _isClobbered check so a form field cannot shadow them.
#
# ATTENTION, and this rank is a different kind from the rest of the block: the
# weakness is inside the sanitizer, so there is no application-side pattern at
# all. Code that calls DOMPurify.sanitize(input) and assigns the result is doing
# the correct thing and is exactly the code that is vulnerable. Any rule keyed on
# that call would report correct usage.
#
# The route that fits is the dependency, and the tool's answer there is wrong in
# a way worth stating: DOMPurify.sanitize emits core.HtmlEscape, a neutralizing
# control, gated only on dependency("dompurify") with no version constraint. On a
# project pinned to 3.1.2, a version this CVE affects, the vulnerable library
# suppresses the XSS finding. Measured on a probe whose package.json pins 3.1.2:
# the sanitized assignment reports nothing and the unsanitized one beside it
# reports VYQL-INJ-004 at high confidence, so the suppression is the control
# doing its job with no idea the library is broken.
#
# Gating that control on a safe version is expressible -- the requirement
# compiler accepts dependency("name", range: "...") -- and is still the wrong
# change, because the gate fails closed on anything but an exact version.
# Measured with a range of >=3.1.3 against four package.json specs: 3.1.2
# rejected, 3.1.3 satisfied, ^3.1.2 rejected, * rejected. A caret range is what
# most projects write, so the version-gated control would be lost on most of
# them and every correctly sanitized assignment would become an XSS report.
#
# The right place is SCA, which reports a vulnerable dependency without touching
# the control, and there the limit is data: vyql/sca/advisories.json holds 15
# advisories over 5 ecosystems, npm having four packages, and dompurify is not
# among them.
#
# verify_current_expected: attention-vocabulary-gap

test "rank1083: sanitized output is not reported, whatever the library version"
  lang javascript
  file app.js
  reject VYQL-INJ-004
  code
  ```
  const DOMPurify = require('dompurify');

  function render(req, el) {
    const raw = req.query.bio;
    el.innerHTML = DOMPurify.sanitize(raw);
  }
  ```

test "rank1083: the same assignment without the sanitizer is reported"
  lang javascript
  file app.js
  expect VYQL-INJ-004
  code
  ```
  function render(req, el) {
    const raw = req.query.bio;
    el.innerHTML = raw;
  }
  ```

# The library's own fix, kept so a later reader can see what the patched
# sanitizer does rather than inferring it from the advisory text.

test "rank1083: the sanitizer bounds nesting depth itself"
  lang javascript
  file src/purify.js
  reject VYQL-INJ-004
  code
  ```
  const MAX_NESTING_DEPTH = 500;

  function _sanitizeElements(currentNode) {
    if (currentNode.nodeType === 1) {
      if (currentNode.parentNode && currentNode.parentNode.__depth) {
        currentNode.__depth =
          (currentNode.__removalCount || 0) + currentNode.parentNode.__depth + 1;
      } else {
        currentNode.__depth = 1;
      }
    }

    if (currentNode.__depth >= MAX_NESTING_DEPTH) {
      _forceRemove(currentNode);
    }
  }
  ```
